{-
   The explicit Unicode tier - Implementation-String.md part 8.

   Everything that needs the Unicode Character Database is here and nowhere in `Core`, so a program
   that never imports this module carries none of its tables, on either target. The answers are the
   library's own and not the host's: a JS build gets the same general category for a character that
   a native build gets, whatever Unicode version its engine happens to ship.

   **The data is generated**, by `tools/unicode/generate.py` from the version `unicodeVersion` names,
   into `Tables.yana` and the other `*Tables.yana` files, with the conformance data the tests walk.
   Nothing in those files is written by hand.

   **Names say what they need.** A predicate named after a Unicode property is that property exactly
   (`isAlphabetic`, `isXidStart`); one that has an ASCII-tier namesake in `Core` carries `Unicode`
   (`isLowercaseUnicode` beside `isLower`), so that a call reads as the tier it is on.

   **Every question is one lookup.** A character's category and all of its binary properties share
   one table entry, so these are three loads and a mask each, the same for ASCII and for everything
   else. White space is the exception, and is `Core`'s `isSpaceUnicode`: its 25 characters are a
   pattern match rather than a table.

   The rest of the module, each over tables of its own:

   - `Clusters.yana` - UAX #29 extended grapheme clusters.
   - `Case.yana` - full case mapping and folding.
   - `Normalization.yana` - UAX #15's four forms, and the NFKC case-folded key for identifiers.
   - `Collation.yana` - UTS #10 over CLDR's root order, as `UnicodeCollator`.
   - `Collator.yana` - `Collator`, which is the host's `Intl.Collator` on JS and `UnicodeCollator`
     natively.
   - `Ascii.native.yana` - the register-at-a-time ASCII paths the others share.
-}

{-
   The `General_Category` of a character: UAX #44's thirty values, under their long names.

   **The order is the UCD's grouping and it is load-bearing:** the category is the constructor
   number in the table (`Tables.yana`), and the group tests below are ranges over it. The generator
   writes numbers in this order, so a constructor moved here is a table that says something else.
-}
pub data qualified GeneralCategory
  = @value(0) UppercaseLetter
  | @value(1) LowercaseLetter
  | @value(2) TitlecaseLetter
  | @value(3) ModifierLetter
  | @value(4) OtherLetter
  | @value(5) NonspacingMark
  | @value(6) SpacingMark
  | @value(7) EnclosingMark
  | @value(8) DecimalNumber
  | @value(9) LetterNumber
  | @value(10) OtherNumber
  | @value(11) ConnectorPunctuation
  | @value(12) DashPunctuation
  | @value(13) OpenPunctuation
  | @value(14) ClosePunctuation
  | @value(15) InitialPunctuation
  | @value(16) FinalPunctuation
  | @value(17) OtherPunctuation
  | @value(18) MathSymbol
  | @value(19) CurrencySymbol
  | @value(20) ModifierSymbol
  | @value(21) OtherSymbol
  | @value(22) SpaceSeparator
  | @value(23) LineSeparator
  | @value(24) ParagraphSeparator
  | @value(25) Control
  | @value(26) Format
  | @value(27) Surrogate
  | @value(28) PrivateUse
  | @value(29) Unassigned

-- `c`'s general category. A value above U+10FFFF, which the `Char` constructor lets through, is
-- `Unassigned`.
pub fn generalCategory(c: Char) -> GeneralCategory = propertyCategories[propertyIndexAt(c) :: Size]

-- The one-letter groups, `L`, `M`, `N`, `P`, `S`, `Z` and `C`: each is a run of constructors above.
pub fn GeneralCategory.isLetter(category: GeneralCategory) -> Bool = valueOf(category) is 0..=4
pub fn GeneralCategory.isMark(category: GeneralCategory) -> Bool = valueOf(category) is 5..=7
pub fn GeneralCategory.isNumber(category: GeneralCategory) -> Bool = valueOf(category) is 8..=10
pub fn GeneralCategory.isPunctuation(category: GeneralCategory) -> Bool = valueOf(category) is 11..=17
pub fn GeneralCategory.isSymbol(category: GeneralCategory) -> Bool = valueOf(category) is 18..=21
pub fn GeneralCategory.isSeparator(category: GeneralCategory) -> Bool = valueOf(category) is 22..=24
pub fn GeneralCategory.isOther(category: GeneralCategory) -> Bool = valueOf(category) is 25..=29

-- Whether any bit of `mask` is set in `c`'s property word.
fn hasProperty(c: Char, mask: U16) -> Bool = (propertyWord(c) `and` mask) != 0

-- The `Alphabetic` property: letters, letter numbers, and the marks and symbols that behave as
-- letters (`Other_Alphabetic`). Wider than the `L` group, since it takes `Ⅻ` and Devanagari's vowel
-- signs.
pub fn isAlphabetic(c: Char) -> Bool = hasProperty(c, alphabeticBit)

-- The `Lowercase` and `Uppercase` properties, which are wider than `Ll` and `Lu`: `ª` and the
-- modifier letters are lowercase, and the circled capitals `Ⓐ` are uppercase.
pub fn isLowercaseUnicode(c: Char) -> Bool = hasProperty(c, lowercaseBit)
pub fn isUppercaseUnicode(c: Char) -> Bool = hasProperty(c, uppercaseBit)

{-
   Whether `c` is a number of any kind: the `N` group, so decimal digits in every script, letter
   numbers such as `Ⅻ`, and other numbers such as `½` and `²`.

   The general category and not `Numeric_Type`, which is also what Rust's `is_numeric` and .NET's
   `IsNumber` answer. The difference is the CJK ideographs that Unihan gives a numeric value (`一`,
   `万`): those are letters that happen to count, and a caller splitting words from numbers wants them
   with the letters.
-}
pub fn isNumericUnicode(c: Char) -> Bool = generalCategoryValue(c) is 8..=10

pub fn isAlphanumericUnicode(c: Char) -> Bool = isAlphabetic(c) || isNumericUnicode(c)

-- `XID_Start` and `XID_Continue`, UAX #31's identifier syntax closed under NFKC: what a language
-- accepting Unicode identifiers takes as the first character of one and as each character after it.
pub fn isXidStart(c: Char) -> Bool = hasProperty(c, xidStartBit)
pub fn isXidContinue(c: Char) -> Bool = hasProperty(c, xidContinueBit)

-- The category's constructor number, for a range test that does not build the constructor.
fn generalCategoryValue(c: Char) -> U16 = propertyWord(c) `and` categoryMask
